{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothLemmaStatement","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothLemmaStatement","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothBaseCase","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Rat.Floor","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Int.Interval"],"declarations":104,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaMahler_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaMahler_one","kind":"theorem","line":2051,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L2051-L2057","doc":"**§14's base case, `Θ₁ ≤ t = c₁t`**, discharged.\n\nThis is the one instance of `RothLemmaMahler` this repository proves.  At one variable the\ngrowth condition is vacuous and `ε = t/1 = t`, so the schema's `m = 1` case supplies `θ = t`\ndirectly — and `rothBound_one` says that is exactly what §14 asks for. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaUniform_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaUniform_one","kind":"theorem","line":2007,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L2007-L2049","doc":"**The `m = 1` case of Roth's lemma**, as an instance of the statement above.\n\nHolds for **every** `ω`, because the growth condition is vacuous when there is only one\nvariable — there is no consecutive pair.\n\n⚠ The `degreeOf` hypothesis is not used: at one variable the height bound alone suffices.\nThat is why it was easy to omit from a draft of the statement, and why it is worth stating\nthat its absence there was an error rather than an economy.\n\nEverything comes from `RothBaseCase.lean`; **no reindexing lemma appears**, which is the\nevidence for the design choice argued in the module header. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothLemmaMahler","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothLemmaMahler","kind":"def","line":1982,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1982-L2005","doc":"**Roth's lemma as Mahler states it** (§14, Theorem 1), assembled from the schema.\n\nThe source carries one small parameter `t` and ties everything to it:\n\n* property `Γ_m`'s first clause is `RothGrowth` with `ω = 1/t` (see `RothGrowth` above);\n* its height clause `a ≤ H₁^{(1/m) r₁ t}` is, in uniform form, `ε = t/m`;\n* §14's conclusion is `Θ_m ≤ c_m·t^{2^{−(m−1)}}`, i.e. `RothBound m t θ`.\n\nThe bound is existential because it must be *uniform* in all the data — one `θ` serving every\n`o, d, p, q, P, B, E` — which is what the source proves and what the `∃` outside\n`RothLemmaUniform`'s own `∀`s expresses.\n\n⚠ Still a `def`.  Nothing in this repository proves it for `m ≥ 2`; `rothLemmaMahler_one` is\nthe only instance discharged.\n\nAll three of `Γ_m`'s clauses are carried, via `RothGamma` inside the schema.  They were not,\nfor most of this arc's life, which made its statements **stronger** than what §14 proves — a\ntheorem stronger than the source's is one nothing in the source will discharge.\n\n⚠ `0 < t ≤ 1` is Theorem 1's opening hypothesis and was missing here until §14 was read in\nfull; §14's computation uses it twice (`s² ≤ s` and `t ≤ s`).  Carried now. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothLemmaUniform","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothLemmaUniform","kind":"def","line":1934,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1934-L1980","doc":"**Roth's lemma, uniform-parameter form** — the statement *schema*.\n\nRead: an integer polynomial of degree at most `dⱼ` in `xⱼ`, of height at most `B`, cannot have\nindex larger than `θ` at a rational point `(pⱼ/qⱼ)` whose denominators are large compared\nto `B`.\n\n⚠ A `def`, not a theorem: only `m = 1` is proved (`rothLemmaUniform_one`).  ⚠ False for small\n`ω` when `m ≥ 2`.  ⚠ A uniform-`E` variant of the textbook statement.  See the module header\nfor all three.\n\n⚠ **The bound `θ` is a parameter, so this is a schema and not a claim.**  Taking `θ` large\nmakes it trivially true.  Roth's lemma is the instance in `RothLemmaMahler` below, where `ω`,\n`ε` and `θ` are all tied to the single parameter `t` the source uses.  Quoting this `def` on\nits own overstates what it says.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction eighteen, second consequence: the point and `Γ`'s parameter are different\nobjects.**  This `def` used to quantify one function `qd : σ → ℤ` and use it *both* as the\ndenominator of the point (`index d (fun j => pn j / qd j)`, `IsCoprime (pn j) (qd j)`) **and**\nas `Γ_m`'s `K_h` (`RothGamma o ω d qd`, `B ≤ qd j ^ E`).  §11 says `K_h = H_h = max(|P_h|,|Q_h|)`,\nand `H ≥ Q` with equality only when the denominator dominates, so the two readings are not\ninterchangeable.\n\nThe old form was **not false** — it is a theorem with a *stronger* hypothesis (`B ≤ Q^E`\nimplies `B ≤ H^E`) and hence a weaker statement.  What it could not be is *fed from `Γ_m`*,\nwhich supplies only `B ≤ H^E`.  Since the induction consumes this schema at `m − 1` and `Γ`\nis what the induction carries, the old form was unusable at exactly the point of use.\n\nNow: the point is a rational `κ : σ → ℚ` (Lean's `ℚ` supplies lowest terms and a positive\ndenominator, so no coprimality clause is needed — §9 states one and the type carries it), and\n`Kh` is pinned to its **height** by the second hypothesis.  Compare `InRothFracSet`, which\ntook the same shape after correction sixteen.\n\n⚠ The `m = 1` proof now goes through `index_le_of_abs_coeff_le` rather than\n`index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le` — i.e. through **both** halves of §10.  It has to: the\n`Q^j` half alone cannot bound `B` by a power of the *height*. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_of_max_form_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_of_max_form_witness","kind":"theorem","line":1926,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1926-L1931","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the junction.**  `m = 1`, `s = 1/4`, `Ψ = 1/8`, `t = 1/16`, `A = q = 1/4`,\n`θ = 1/16 + 1/2 = 9/16`; the conclusion is `RothBound 2 (1/16) (9/16)`, a real bound. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_descend_step_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_descend_step_witness","kind":"theorem","line":1917,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1917-L1924","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the height step.**  `m = 2`, `ρ₁ = 1`, `Λ = 1`, `t = 1`, `L = 30`,\n`B = 15`: clause 3 reads `1·2·5·1 = 10 ≤ 30` and the conclusion `1·15 ≤ 30`.\n\n⚠ `L = 30` against `Λ = 1` is not slack — at `m = 2` clause 3 demands `H₁ ≥ 2^{10}`, and\n`rothHeightFloor_two` says the same.  The witness is at the boundary of what `Γ₂` allows. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_descend_pq_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_descend_pq_witness","kind":"theorem","line":1905,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1905-L1915","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the exact descent, at the calibration boundary.**\n\n`a = 1`, `b = 32`, `K = 1024 = 2^10`, `m = n = r₁ = ρ₁ = 1`, `c = 5`, `F = 10`, `p = q = 1`\n(so `t = 1`).  ⚠ **Three of the hypotheses hold with equality**: `2^F = K`, `c = (2m+3)ρ₁`, and\n`q·m(m+1)(2m+3) = F·p`.  The witness sits exactly where clause 3's calibration puts it, which\nis the concrete form of \"nothing to spare\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_descend_pq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_descend_pq","kind":"theorem","line":1855,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1855-L1903","doc":"**Clause 4's descent, integral and exact** — `t = p/q`, so nothing is rounded.\n\nClause 4 `b ≤ K₁^{(1/M)s₁t}` raised to the power `Mq` is `b^{Mq} ≤ K₁^{s₁p}`, which is a\nstatement about integers.  Descending from arity `m+1` to `m`, §11's chain becomes a chain of\n`ℕ`-exponent inequalities and closes **exactly**, with no interval for `j` to fall out of:\n\n    b^{m(m+1)q} ≤ 2^{c·m(m+1)q}·(a^{(m+1)q})^{nm} ≤ K^{ρ₁p}·K^{ρ₁pm} = (K^{ρ₁p})^{m+1},\n\nthe middle step needing `c·m(m+1)q ≤ F·ρ₁p`, which is `c ≤ (2m+3)ρ₁` and clause 3's\n`q·m(m+1)(2m+3) ≤ F·p` multiplied together.  Both are `ℕ` inequalities, so the tightness that\nblocked the rounded route costs nothing here.\n\n⚠ This is the route the section above recommends and does not take.  It is taken here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_descend_exp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_descend_exp","kind":"theorem","line":1803,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1803-L1828","doc":"**Clause 4's multiplicative half, log-free** — the arc's `E : ℕ` idiom rather than logs.\n\n`RothLemmaUniform` already avoids rational exponents by writing the height condition as\n`B ≤ qⱼ^E` with `E : ℕ` and `E ≤ ε·dⱼ`.  Clause 4 in that idiom is `a ≤ K^E`, and §11's chain\nbecomes: `b ≤ 2^c·a^n` with the `2^c` absorbed into `K^j` through clause 3's `2^F ≤ K`.\n\nEverything stays in `ℤ`.  `F` is `rothHeightFloor`'s exponent and `j` the number of powers of\n`K` needed to swallow `2^c`; the arithmetic that says `j` is small enough is\n`height_descend_step`, in `ℚ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_descend_assemble","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_descend_assemble","kind":"theorem","line":1785,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1785-L1801","doc":"**The assembly** — `b = 2^{2n∑r}·n!·a^n` bounded, in logs.\n\n`Fac` stands for `log n!`, bounded by `ρ₁·Λ` through `n! ≤ n^n ≤ 2^{n r₁}`; `sumR` for\n`r₁ + … + r_m`, bounded by `m·r₁`; and `A` for `log a`, bounded by clause 4 of `Γ_m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.height_descend_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.height_descend_step","kind":"theorem","line":1758,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1758-L1783","doc":"**The calibration step** — and where `Γ_m`'s clause 3 finally earns its exponent.\n\n⚠ The gap between the two exponents is `1/(m−1) − 1/m = 1/(m(m−1))`, so absorbing the\naccumulated `2^{(2m+1)ρ₁}` needs `H₁^{ρ₁t/(m(m−1))} ≥ 2^{(2m+1)ρ₁}`, i.e.\n\n    t·log H₁  ≥  m(m−1)(2m+1)·log 2,\n\nwhich is clause 3 — `K₁ ≥ 2^{(1/t)(m−1)m(2m+1)}` — with **nothing to spare**.  That is why the\nfloor's exponent is `(1/t)(m−1)m(2m+1)` and not some rounder constant: `m(m−1)` cancels the\nexponent gap and `(2m+1)` matches what `2^{2mρ₁}·2^{ρ₁}` accumulates.\n\nThis arc carried clause 3 for several turns without any use for it, and recorded twice that it\nwas \"the first place clauses 2 and 3 have to be used\".  This is that place, and the fit is\nexact rather than generous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.theta_one_le_of_gamma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.theta_one_le_of_gamma","kind":"theorem","line":1708,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1708-L1739","doc":"**§13's discharge of `Θ₁ ≤ t` from `Γ_m`**, with the logarithms carried as variables.\n\n§10 gives `Θ₁(b; ρ_m; H_m) ≤ log b / (ρ_m log H_m)`.  Then `Γ_m` clause 2 — `r_m log H_m ≥\nr₁ log H₁`, hence `ρ_m log H_m ≥ ρ₁ log H₁` since `ρ_h = n·r_h` — lets the denominator be\nreplaced, and §11's `b ≤ H₁^{(1/(m−1))ρ₁t}` bounds the numerator.  What comes out is\n`t/(m−1) ≤ t`, using `m ≥ 2`.\n\n⚠ `logb`, `L₁`, `Lm` stand for `log b`, `log H₁`, `log H_m`; nothing here is about logarithms,\nonly about the inequalities they satisfy.  That keeps the step in `ℚ` and matches how the rest\nof this arc handles the height chain.\n\n⚠ **`0 ≤ log b` is not a hypothesis, and the reason is worth naming.**  The source's route\nreplaces the denominator first — `log b/(ρ_m log H_m) ≤ log b/(ρ₁ log H₁)` — and *that* needs\n`log b ≥ 0`.  The proof below never forms that quotient: it bounds `log b` above and multiplies\nthrough, so the positivity never arises.\n\nThat is the fourth hypothesis this arc has written down and then found unused (`0 ≤ u`,\n`0 ≤ Φ`/`0 ≤ D₂`, `0 ≤ w`, and now this), and all four came from transcribing the *paper's*\nproof route rather than the route Lean actually takes.  Worth watching for: the linter is\ncatching a specific habit, not random slack. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_mul_of_factor_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_mul_of_factor_le","kind":"theorem","line":1700,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1700-L1706","doc":"**The junction condition at the source's normalisation** — free, given `Φ ≤ Ψ`.\n\n`S = r·Φ` and `rr = r`, so `S ≤ Ψ·rr` is `r·Φ ≤ Ψ·r`.  No largeness requirement, no condition\non the split-off degree.  Contrast `factor_bound_le_of_split_degree` above, which is what the\nmis-transcribed `r_m` cost. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.factor_le_of_theta_one_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.factor_le_of_theta_one_le","kind":"theorem","line":1696,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1696-L1698","doc":"**`Φ ≤ Ψ` reduces to `Θ₁ ≤ t`.**  `Φ_m = Θ' + Θ₁` and `Ψ_m = Θ' + t`, so the two differ\nonly in the `y`-side term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.radicand_weaken","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.radicand_weaken","kind":"theorem","line":1667,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1667-L1694","doc":"**Mahler's weakening of the radicand**, display (2) on p. 95: `√((2/r_m)·S) ≤ 2·√(S/n)`.\n\nThis is the step that carries §13 from its sharp intermediate — radicand `(2/r_m)·S`, which is\nalso what this arc's own `le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le` derives — to the packaged form with\nradicand `S/n`.  The source's chain is\n`r_m/2 ≥ (r_m/(r_m+1))·(n/2) ≥ (1/2)·(n/2) = n/4`; all it actually needs is **`n ≤ 2·r_m`**,\nwhich `n ≤ r_m + 1` and `r_m ≥ 1` give at once.\n\nRoot-free: `v` stands for the left square root and `w` for `√(S/n)`.\n\n⚠ `hw` is `S/n ≤ w²`, not `S/n = w²` — correction 25.  The `=` form demands that `S/n` be a\nsquare in `ℚ`, which nothing supplies, and only the `≤` direction is used.  `0 ≤ S` was\npreviously *derived* from the equality and so has to be assumed now; it is free at every call\nsite (`S` is a bound on an index). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.factor_bound_le_of_split_degree_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.factor_bound_le_of_split_degree_eq","kind":"theorem","line":1614,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1614-L1617","doc":"**The best case `D₂ = d₂`**, where the requirement is simply `Φ ≤ t·d₂`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.factor_bound_le_of_split_degree","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.factor_bound_le_of_split_degree","kind":"theorem","line":1563,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1563-L1612","doc":"**What the junction's condition becomes at the call site** — the numeric requirement, made\nfully explicit.\n\n`RothOperatorFamily.index_le_of_rothGrowth_of_factor_bound` carries §13's hypothesis in the\nform `(d₂ + 1)·Φ ≤ q²·D₂`, where `Φ` bounds each factor's index, `d₂` is the split-off\nvariable's degree and `D₂ ≤ d₂` a floor for it.  `rothBound_of_max_form` adds `q² ≤ Ψ`.\nChaining them through `le_mul_of_max_form_squeeze` gives `(d₂ + 1)·Φ ≤ Ψ·D₂`, and with §14's\n`Ψ = Θ_{m−1} + t = Φ + t` that rearranges to exactly the hypothesis below.\n\n⚠ **It is a largeness requirement on the split-off degree, not a bookkeeping identity.**  At\nthe best case `D₂ = d₂` it reads `Φ ≤ t·d₂` (`factor_bound_le_of_split_degree_eq`).\n\nWhat `Φ` is, precisely: `RothInductionStep.factor_bound_of_rescaled` bounds the two\nWronskians' summed index at the *unscaled* degrees by `r·(εU + εV)`, so the arc's `Φ` is\n`εU + εV` — the **sum** of the two halves' bounds, hence `Φ ≤ 2Θ_{m−1}`, not `Θ_{m−1}`.  The\n`r` is the rescaling factor and cancels against §13's `S/r`; it is not a row count entering\nthe size of `Φ`.  Since `Θ_{m−1} ≤ c_{m−1}·t^{2^{−(m−2)}}` is for small `t` much larger than\n`t`, `d₂` must be at least `Φ/t`.\n\n⚠⚠ **Superseded** — the requirement does not arise at the source's `n` normalisation, which\n§13 reaches from the `r_m` one by a proved weakening (`radicand_weaken`).  See corrections\nfourteen and fifteen below; fourteen got the *reason* wrong and fifteen repairs it, but the\nconclusion that this requirement is an artifact stands.  Kept because this arc keeps its\ncorrections visible rather than deleting them.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction thirteen: this is a cost of the arc's own §13 formulation, not a hypothesis of\nRoth's lemma.**  §13's packaged conclusion is Mahler's **Lemma 4**, now read:\n\n    Θ_m ≤ t + 2·max(Ψ_m, √Ψ_m),      Ψ_m = Θ_{m−1} + t,\n\ngiven `Γ_m`, and producing a system with `Γ_{m−1}`.  Its `max` is over **one** quantity and its\nsquare root — no `/n`, no `/r_m`, and **no side condition**.  The arc's\n`index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound` instead has `max(S/r, q)` with `S ≤ q²·rr`, which is where\n`(d₂ + 1 − D₂)·Φ ≤ t·D₂` comes from.\n\nSo the requirement below is real for the arc's route and must be discharged, but it is **not**\nan extra hypothesis of Roth's lemma: whatever `S ≤ q²·rr` costs is discharged from `Γ_m` inside\n§13's proof.  ⚠ That proof body has not been read, so *how* is still open — this says only that\nit is not a gap in the theorem.  The previous version of this docstring presented the condition\nas the arc's open parameter question; that framing was wrong.\n\n`Γ_m`'s clauses 2 and 3 are still what any discharge must use, and this remains the first point\nin the arc where they are needed — nothing earlier needed them, which is exactly why they went\nmissing for so long.\n\n⚠ Neither positivity of `Φ` nor of `D₂` is a hypothesis — the rearrangement is linear in the\nmonomials.  Same reason `0 ≤ u` came off `rothConstant_step`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_mul_of_max_form_squeeze","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_mul_of_max_form_squeeze","kind":"theorem","line":1544,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1544-L1561","doc":"⚠ **The junction squeezes `q` from both sides**, and that is the whole numeric content.\n\n§13 needs `S ≤ q²·rr` — a **lower** bound on `q²`, since `q` is its rational stand-in for\n`√(S/rr)`.  `rothBound_of_max_form` needs `q² ≤ Ψ` — an **upper** bound.  Together they force\n\n    S ≤ Ψ·rr,\n\nand that single inequality is what the §13 → §14 junction reduces to.  The bean for this arc\nhad said the assembly \"terminates in a numeric condition\"; this is that condition, isolated to\none place rather than diffused through the recursion.\n\n⚠ It is necessary, not sufficient: a rational `q` with `S/rr ≤ q² ≤ Ψ` exists whenever\n`S/rr < Ψ` (rational squares are dense), but at equality it exists only if `Ψ` is a rational\nsquare.  The strict form is what a caller should establish; that density argument is not\nproved here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_of_max_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_of_max_form","kind":"theorem","line":1525,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1525-L1542","doc":"**§13's conclusion feeds §14's step** — the junction, with every linking hypothesis named.\n\n`RothInductionStep.index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound` ends at `θ ≤ t + 2·max(S/r, q)`.  That is\n`rothBound_step`'s `θ ≤ Ψ + u` with `u = 2·max(S/r, q)`, provided `t ≤ Ψ` — which holds because\n§14's `Ψ = Θ_{m−1} + t` and `Θ_{m−1} ≥ 0` — and provided both branches of the max are bounded\nby `√Ψ`, which is what `A² ≤ Ψ` and `q² ≤ Ψ` say without naming a root.\n\n`A` is `S/r` at the call site; it is left abstract because nothing here needs it to be a\nquotient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_step_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_step_witness","kind":"theorem","line":1517,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1517-L1523","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the step.**  `m = 1`, `s = 1/4`, `Ψ = 1/8`, `u = 1/2`, `θ = 5/8`: every\nhypothesis holds and the conclusion is a real bound.  ⚠ A finite witness tests vacuity, not\nusefulness — this arc has said so before (correction 9) and it is still true here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_step","kind":"theorem","line":1501,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1501-L1515","doc":"**The induction step in `RothBound` shape** — the obligation the recursion has to meet.\n\nFrom the sub-problem's bound and §13's inequality, the assembled bound satisfies `RothBound` at\nthe next arity.  This is the statement an `m ≥ 2` proof has to reach; everything else in the\narc feeds `Ψ` and `u`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_chain_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_chain_witness","kind":"theorem","line":1489,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1489-L1499","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the chain**, at the constant sequence `sq ≡ 1`, `t = 1`.\n\n⚠ Degenerate on purpose: `sq ≡ 1` satisfies `sq (n+1)² = sq n` and `t ≤ sq n` trivially, so\nthis checks that the seven hypotheses can hold together at all.  The interesting instances have\n`sq n = s^{2^{m−n}}` for a small `s`, where the chain is not constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.crude_chain_fails","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.crude_chain_fails","kind":"theorem","line":1472,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1472-L1487","doc":"⚠ **Why the chain is parameterised by `s` and not by `t` alone.**\n\nThe obvious attempt is to run the induction directly on `RothBound`'s squared form, never\nnaming `s`.  Writing `k = 2^{n−1}`, one has `Θ'^k ≤ c_n^k·t` and wants `Θ^{2k} ≤ c_{n+1}^{2k}·t`\nfrom `Θ ≤ t + 2w`, `w² = Θ' + t`.  Bounding `(a+b)^k ≤ 2^k(a^k + b^k)` twice and using `t ≤ 1`\ngives\n\n    Θ^{2k} ≤ 4^k·(8^k·(c_n^k + 1) + 1)·t,\n\nwhose leading behaviour is `(32·c_n)^k`, so the route needs `32·c_n ≤ (2c_n + 3)^2`.  At\n`c_n = 1` that is `32 ≤ 25` — **false**, and `c₁ = 1`, so it fails at the very first step.\n\n⚠ This does **not** prove no root-free route exists; it shows the natural crude one loses too\nmuch.  That is the same `√`-slack failure as everywhere else in this arc, and it is why the\nchain above names `s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_chain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_chain","kind":"theorem","line":1459,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1459-L1470","doc":"**The chain in `RothBound` shape.**  Same hypotheses, conclusion converted by\n`rothBound_pow_iff` at each level. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_chain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_chain","kind":"theorem","line":1398,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1398-L1457","doc":"**§14's induction, as pure arithmetic** — the chain, closed.\n\nGiven the step at every level, `Θ_n ≤ c_n·s_n` propagates from the base case all the way up.\nThe sequence `sq` is `s_n`, each the square root of the previous (`sq (n+1)² = sq n`), which is\nwhat `rothBound_pow_iff` needs at each level and what keeps the whole chain at a single `t`.\n\n⚠ **`t ≤ sq n` at every level is a real hypothesis, not bookkeeping.**  It is what lets `Ψ`\nabsorb the additive `t`, and it is exactly `t ≤ s^{2^k}` for the relevant `k` — true because\n`0 ≤ s ≤ 1`, but it has to be supplied.\n\n⚠ **The chain needs the `s_n` to exist as rationals.**  For a general rational `t` they do not:\n`s_n = t^{2^{−(n−1)}}` is irrational in general.  So this closes the induction exactly for `t`\nthat is a perfect `2^{m−1}`-th power of a rational — which is a genuine restriction, and the\nprice of staying in `ℚ`.  Going to `ℝ` for the exponent is the alternative; it is not taken. \n⚠⚠ **Correction twenty-three: the ranges used to be unbounded, and that made this vacuous.**\n\n`hsq` read `∀ n, sq (n + 1) ^ 2 = sq n` — for **every** `n`.  Iterating, `sq 1 = sq (k+1)^{2^k}`\nfor every `k`, so `sq 1` is a `2^k`-th power **in `ℚ`** for every `k`.  Writing `sq 1 = a/b` in\nlowest terms, each `a` and `b` is then a `2^k`-th power for every `k`, so every prime exponent in\nthem is divisible by every `2^k` and hence zero: `sq 1 = 1` (or `0`, forcing `sq ≡ 0` and\n`t ≤ 0`).  At `sq 1 = 1` the whole sequence is `1` and the conclusion is `Θ n ≤ c_n`, with **no\n`t` in it at all** — not §14's `Θ_m ≤ c_m·t^{2^{−(m−1)}}`.\n\nSo the only instance was the constant one the witness below exhibits, and the witness's own note\n— \"the interesting instances have `sq n = s^{2^{m−n}}`\" — describes a sequence that is not\ndefined for `n > m` and therefore never satisfied `hsq`.\n\nBounding the ranges to `n < M` fixes it: only `M` successive square roots are needed, so\n`sq 1 = t` may be any `2^{M−1}`-th power of a rational, which is what an application choosing its\nown `t` would supply anyway.\n\n⚠ The arithmetic above is elementary but **is not formalised here**.  A Lean witness would need\nthe irrationality of `√2` (`Mathlib.Data.Real.Irrational`), whose `.olean` is not in this\nproject's built Mathlib closure, so importing it would mean compiling that module.  Recorded as\nan argument, not as a theorem.\n\n⚠ The restriction to perfect powers is an artifact of the **root-free rational encoding**, not\nof §14 — over `ℝ` every non-negative `t` has a `2^k`-th root and the restriction vanishes.\nRemoving it in `ℚ` means proving the step by passing to `ℝ` and back, which the inequalities all\nsupport (they are field-generic) but which is not done here.\n","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.two_pow_one_eq_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.two_pow_one_eq_two","kind":"theorem","line":1390,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1390-L1396","doc":"⚠ **At `m = 2` the unperturbed hypothesis has no room at all.**\n\n`Ψ ≤ (c+1)s²` is reached from `Θ_{m−1} ≤ c·s²` and `t ≤ s²`, and `t = s^{2^{m−1}}`, so at\n`m = 2` that reads `t = s²` — an **equality**.  Any `δ > 0` added to the base case exceeds the\nbudget immediately, which is why `rothConstant_step_perturbed` has to carry `v` in its\nconclusion rather than absorb it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.two_sqrt_fixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.two_sqrt_fixed","kind":"theorem","line":1387,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1387-L1388","doc":"**And `4` is its fixed point** — so the amplification stops only where the bound is useless. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.two_sqrt_expands","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.two_sqrt_expands","kind":"theorem","line":1380,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1380-L1385","doc":"**The slack recursion expands**: `2√x > x` for `0 < x < 4`, root-free.\n\n`y = 2√x` is `y ≥ 0 ∧ y² = 4x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step_perturbed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step_perturbed","kind":"theorem","line":1314,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1314-L1350","doc":"**§14's step with the base case perturbed by `δ`** — what the `1/d` rounding costs.\n\nIf the base case yields `Θ₁ ≤ t + δ` instead of `Θ₁ ≤ t` — which is exactly what migrating\n`RothLemmaUniform` onto clause 4's `p/q` form would produce, with `δ = 1/d` — then `Ψ` is\nbounded by `(c+1)s² + δ` rather than `(c+1)s²`, and the step's conclusion picks up `2v` where\n`v = √δ`.\n\nBoth branches of the max still land on `(c+1)s + v`:\n\n* `w = Ψ ≤ (c+1)s² + v² ≤ (c+1)s + v`, using `s² ≤ s` and `v² ≤ v`;\n* `w² = Ψ ≤ (c+1)s² + v² ≤ ((c+1)s + v)²`, since `(c+1)² ≥ c+1` and the cross term is\n  non-negative.\n\n⚠ **The perturbation does not fit inside the constant.**  `2c+3` is what both branches give\nwith the crude bounds already exactly tight at `c = 0`, so there is no smaller admissible\nconstant to trade against `2v`.  The invariant would have to widen from `θ ≤ c_m·s` to\n`θ ≤ c_m·s + κ` and the recursion be re-derived for `κ` — a different theorem, not a looser\nconstant in this one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step_lemma4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step_lemma4","kind":"theorem","line":1245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1245-L1312","doc":"**§14's step exactly as printed** — Lemma 4's shape, and the faithful one.\n\nLemma 4 (§13's packaged conclusion, now **read**) is\n\n    Θ_m ≤ t + 2·max(Ψ_m, √Ψ_m),      Ψ_m = Θ_{m−1} + t,\n\ngiven property `Γ_m`, and it **produces** a system with `Γ_{m−1}`.  ⚠ No side condition.\n\n§14 then bounds both branches of that max by the *same* quantity `(c+1)s`, which is why the\n`max` disappears and why the constant is what it is:\n\n* `Ψ ≤ c·s² + t ≤ c·s + s = (c+1)s`, using `s² ≤ s` and `t ≤ s`;\n* `√Ψ ≤ (c+1)s`, because `Ψ ≤ (c+1)s² ≤ (c+1)²s²` — the crude `c+1 ≤ (c+1)²`, valid since\n  `c ≥ 1` (`rothConstant_one_le`, which is Mahler's own standing hypothesis).\n\nLeading `t ≤ s` contributes `1`, the max contributes `2(c+1)`, total `2c+3`.\n\n`w` is `max(Ψ, √Ψ)`, encoded root-free as `w ≤ Ψ ∨ w² ≤ Ψ`.  ⚠ `0 ≤ w` is deliberately **not**\nrequired, though the max satisfies it: without it the second disjunct also admits `−√Ψ`, and a\nsmaller `w` only makes `s + 2w` smaller, so the theorem is stronger without it.  Same reason\n`0 ≤ u` came off `rothConstant_step`.  Callers instantiate at the real max, where it holds.\n\n⚠⚠ **The twenty-fifth correction is the `≤` in that disjunction; it used to be `=`.**  The two\nencodings are not interchangeable, and the `=` one is not the faithful reading of\n`θ ≤ t + 2·max(Ψ, √Ψ)`:\n\n* with `≤`, the admissible `w` are exactly `w ≤ max(Ψ, √Ψ)`, so `∃ w` with `θ ≤ t + 2w` says\n  precisely `θ ≤ t + 2·max(Ψ, √Ψ)`;\n* with `=`, the admissible `w` are `Ψ` and — **only when `Ψ` is a square in `ℚ`** — `±√Ψ`.\n\nSo the `=` form silently demands that `Ψ` be a rational square whenever the `√` branch is the\noperative one, which is the regime the recursion actually runs in (`Ψ < 1`).  Nothing supplies\nthat: `Ψ = Θ_{m−1} + t` with `Θ_{m−1}` an infimum over a finite set of indices and `t = p/q`.\nThe producer of this disjunction is `RothVariableSplit.RothLemma4`, which is why the `def`\nmoved with it.\n\n⚠ Note what the `=` form cost and what it did not.  Nothing proved was false — this theorem is\ntrue either way, and *stronger* now that its hypothesis is weaker.  The damage was entirely on\nthe other side of the arrow: `RothLemma4` had to **produce** a `w`, and could not.  A hypothesis\nnobody has had to discharge is indistinguishable from an unsatisfiable one — correction 9's\nlesson, and this is its fourth instance in the arc.\n\n⚠ This covers what `rothConstant_step_source` does not — that one's `u² ≤ 4Ψ` is the `√Ψ`\nbranch alone, which is the max only when `Ψ ≤ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_one_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_one_le","kind":"theorem","line":1238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1238-L1243","doc":"`c_m ≥ 1` for `m ≥ 1` — Mahler states this as a standing fact in §14's computation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step_source","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step_source","kind":"theorem","line":1213,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1213-L1236","doc":"**§14's own split of the same step** — a different route to the same constant.\n\nThe source's display leads with the row cost (weakened from `t` to `s`) rather than with `Ψ`,\nand bounds `2√(c+1)` by the crude `2c+2` where `rothConstant_step` uses the sharper `c+2`.\nBoth land on `2c+3`; this is the checked half of correction twelve.\n\n⚠ Note this route needs **no** `s ≤ 1`.  `rothConstant_step` does, for `s² ≤ s`.  (Getting\n`Ψ ≤ (c+1)s²` in the first place needs it either way, so nothing is gained — but the\ndifference is real and shows the two splits are not notational variants of one argument.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_step","kind":"theorem","line":1154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1154-L1211","doc":"**§14's induction step, root-free** — and where `c_m = 2c_{m−1} + 3` comes from.\n\nThe source's step is `Ψ_m = Θ_{m−1} + t`, then §13's recursive inequality\n`Θ_m ≤ Ψ_m + 2·max(Ψ_m/n, √(Ψ_m/r_m))`.  Here `u` stands for that whole `2·max(…)` term, and\nthe only thing the step needs of it is `u² ≤ 4Ψ` — which both branches satisfy, since `n`,\n`r_m ≥ 1` and `Ψ ≤ 1` make `max(Ψ/n, √(Ψ/r_m)) ≤ √Ψ`.  Carrying it as `u` with a squared\nhypothesis keeps the whole step inside `ℚ`, which is also the shape `RothRecursionArith` is in.\n\nThe two contributions:\n\n* `Ψ ≤ (c+1)s² ≤ (c+1)s`, using `s ≤ 1` — this is where `Θ_{m−1} ≤ c·s²` and `t ≤ s²` combine;\n* `u ≤ (c+2)s`, because `((c+2)s)² − 4(c+1)s² = c²s² ≥ 0`.\n\nSum: `(2c+3)s`.  ⚠ Note the second bound is the *crude* one, `2√(c+1) ≤ c+2`, and it is\n**exactly tight at `c = 0`**.  So §14's constant is not slack that a sharper argument could\nshave; it is what this inequality gives.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction twelve: an earlier version of this docstring claimed the reading was \"forced\nby the answer\".  It is not, and the claim is withdrawn.**\n\nWhat it said was: §13's `t` is a *row cost* and §11's is the parameter, `Ψ_m = Θ_{m−1} + t`\ncloses to `2c+3` only under the parameter reading, so the constant identifies the reading.\nThe second clause is false.  §14's own display splits the same total differently — leading\nterm `s` (the row cost weakened), and `2√(c+1)` bounded by the cruder `2c+2` — and lands on\n`2c+3` as well:\n\n    §14:        1     + (2c+2)  =  2c+3\n    this proof: (c+1) + (c+2)   =  2c+3\n\n`rothConstant_step_source` below proves that route, so the agreement is a **checked fact** and\nnot an assertion.  Two distinct splits reaching the same constant means reproducing the\nconstant discriminates nothing.\n\nSo: the hypotheses here are **stated, not derived**.  The theorem is true whatever `Ψ` and `u`\nmean, and which quantity §14's `Ψ_m = Θ_{m−1} + t` refers to remains an unverified\nreconstruction.  That is the honest status, and the previous docstring overstated it.\n\n⚠ **`0 ≤ u` is not a hypothesis**, though the quantity it stands for is non-negative.  The\nsquared bound alone gives `u ≤ (c+2)s`: a negative `u` clears a non-negative right-hand side\noutright.  Dropped rather than carried, because this arc has twice found that a hypothesis\nnobody has to discharge is indistinguishable from a false one (corrections 9 and 10) — the\nlinter flagging it unused is the same signal arriving cheaply. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_pow_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_pow_iff","kind":"theorem","line":1139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1139-L1152","doc":"**`RothBound` at a perfect power is a linear bound.**\n\nWith `t = s^{2^{m−1}}` — the substitution `s = t^{2^{−(m−1)}}` read backwards — `RothBound m t θ`\nsays exactly `θ ≤ c_m·s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.two_mul_lt_rothBound_two_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.two_mul_lt_rothBound_two_witness","kind":"theorem","line":1127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1127-L1127","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_two_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_two_half","kind":"theorem","line":1124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1124-L1125","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_one_of_gamma_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_one_of_gamma_one","kind":"theorem","line":1099,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1099-L1114","doc":"**Roth's lemma at `m = 1`, in the arc's own encoding: `Γ₁ ⟹ RothBound 1 t Θ₁`.**\n\n`rothBound_one` unfolds `RothBound 1 t θ` to `0 ≤ θ ∧ θ ≤ t`, so this is `isRothTheta_le_of_gamma_one`\npaired with `IsRothTheta.nonneg`.  Its value is that it is the shape `rothBound_chain` and\n`rothConstant_chain` consume: the base case now enters the recursion as a `RothBound`, not as a\nstatement about `index` that something else would have to translate.\n\n⚠ **`t` is the exact bound at `m = 1`, and that is what pins `c₁ = 1`.**  `rothConstant_rec`\ndoes not determine the constant — `rothConstant_chain` needs a starting value, and correction\neleven traced `c_m = 2^{m+1} − 3` to the base case rather than to the recurrence.  This theorem\nis that starting value, now proved from `Γ₁` rather than assumed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothBound.mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothBound.mono","kind":"theorem","line":1094,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1094-L1097","doc":"Anything smaller than an admissible bound is admissible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_two","kind":"theorem","line":1090,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1090-L1092","doc":"At two variables the bound is `θ² ≤ 25t`, i.e. `θ ≤ 5√t`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_one","kind":"theorem","line":1086,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1086-L1088","doc":"At one variable the bound is `θ ≤ t`, which is §14's base case `Θ₁ ≤ t`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothBound","kind":"def","line":1065,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1065-L1084","doc":"**The bound §14 actually proves**, written without roots.\n\nMahler's conclusion is `Θ_m ≤ c_m · t^{2^{−(m−1)}}`.  The index is `ℚ`-valued and that bound\nis irrational in general, so `RothBound m t θ` states the equivalent inequality obtained by\nraising both non-negative sides to the power `2^{m−1}`:\n\n    0 ≤ θ    and    θ^{2^{m−1}} ≤ c_m^{2^{m−1}} · t.\n\nEquivalent, not weaker: `index ≤ c_m·t^{2^{−(m−1)}}` holds iff some rational `θ` satisfies\n`RothBound m t θ` and bounds the index, by density of `ℚ`.\n\nThe root-free form is also the shape the recursion is already in.  §13's branch is a *square\nroot*, so the arithmetic this arc proved for it — `sq_index_le_of_det_bound`,\n`sq_mul_le_two_mul_of_sum_le` — is stated squared; raising §14's conclusion to a power is\nwhat makes the two meet.\n\n⚠ Only `1 ≤ m` is meaningful.  At `m = 0` the truncated `m − 1` and `rothConstant 0 = 0`\ncollapse this to `θ ≤ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_two","kind":"theorem","line":1062,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1062-L1063","doc":"`c₂ = 5`, the first value where the two closed forms disagree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.two_pow_sub_three_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.two_pow_sub_three_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":1058,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1058-L1060","doc":"⚠ **… and fails the base case, which is what actually rules it out.**  §14's `c₁ = 1` is the\nonly part of the derivation that distinguishes `2^{m+1} − 3` from `2^m − 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.two_pow_sub_three_rec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.two_pow_sub_three_rec","kind":"theorem","line":1051,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1051-L1056","doc":"⚠ **The discarded value satisfies the recurrence.**  Landed as the record of the eleventh\ncorrection, so the wrong constant stays visible rather than being quietly replaced — and so\nthat the reason it survived stays visible with it.  Stated over `ℤ`: at `m = 0` the value\n`2^0 − 3` is negative, and `ℕ` subtraction would truncate the counterexample away. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_rec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_rec","kind":"theorem","line":1036,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1036-L1049","doc":"**The recurrence the closed form must satisfy**, checked rather than asserted.\n\n⚠ This is necessary but **not** sufficient to identify the constant: the recurrence's general\nsolution is `A·2^m − 3`, so the discarded value `2^m − 3` satisfies it too\n(`two_pow_sub_three_rec`).  `rothConstant_one` is what does the separating. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant_one","kind":"theorem","line":1033,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1033-L1034","doc":"The base case `c₁ = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothConstant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothConstant","kind":"def","line":1029,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L1029-L1031","doc":"**Mahler's `c_m`, closed form.**  §14 gives the recurrence `c_m = 2c_{m−1} + 3` with\n`c₁ = 1`; this is its solution. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGamma_scale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGamma_scale","kind":"theorem","line":966,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L966-L985","doc":"**Rescaling the degree vector preserves `Γ_m`** — the companion to `rothGrowth_scale`.\n\nThe induction step measures its sub-problems against `r·d`, and all three clauses survive.\nClause 2 does so because raising `K₁^{s₁} ≤ K_h^{s_h}` to the `r`-th power is monotone once the\nheights are non-negative, which `2 ≤ Kh j` supplies; clause 3 does not mention `d` at\nall. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.toRothGamma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.toRothGamma","kind":"theorem","line":917,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L917-L942","doc":"**`Γ_M` in full implies the three-clause form** — so `RothGammaFull` feeds everything\n`RothGamma` feeds, at `ω = q/p`.\n\nThe only content is clause 3: `rothHeightFloor m (q/p)` rounds `(q/p)(m−1)m(2m+1)` **up**, while\n`floorExp` says `F` is already at least that value, so the ceiling is at most `F` and the\nrounded floor is at most `2^F`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_pow_of_pow_le_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_pow_of_pow_le_pow","kind":"theorem","line":903,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L903-L915","doc":"**From a `p/q` power bound to an `E : ℕ` one** — the bridge between the arc's two height\nidioms, with the rounding as an explicit hypothesis.\n\n`RothGammaFull`'s clause 4 is `b^{Mq} ≤ K₁^{s₁p}`; `RothBaseCase.index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le`\nwants `|lead| ≤ K^e` with `e : ℕ`.  This converts one to the other given `S ≤ Q·e`, which is\n`e ≥ S/Q` written without a ceiling — the same device as `height_descend_exp`'s `j`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.rename_equiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.rename_equiv","kind":"theorem","line":879,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L879-L901","doc":"**`RothGammaFull` transports along a type equivalence.**\n\nEvery clause is stated through `o.symm j`, so reindexing the variable type composes the\nequivalence into the ordering and leaves the content alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.degPos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.degPos","kind":"theorem","line":877,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L877-L877","doc":"§11's standing setting: `s₁,…,s_M, K₁,…,K_M` are **positive** integers.\n\n⚠ Also absent, and also unsafe in the same direction: without it `d j = 0` is admitted, where\n`idxWeight` reads `n/0 = 0` in `ℚ` and the index collapses.  A hypothesis structure missing a\nclause is a *weaker* hypothesis, hence a *stronger* theorem — the shape this arc has now got\nwrong four times. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.two_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.two_le","kind":"theorem","line":870,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L870-L870","doc":"§11's derived list: `K₁ ≥ 2, K₂ ≥ 2, …, K_M ≥ 2`.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction twenty.**  This clause was absent, and its absence made `RothLemmaTheta`\n**false at `m = 1`** — see the counterexample recorded there.  At `m ≥ 2` it is a\n*consequence* of clauses 3a/3b (`(m−1)m(2m+1) ≥ 10 > 0` forces `F ≥ 1`, so `2 ≤ 2^F ≤ K₁`,\nand clause 2 carries it along), so adding it costs case (ii) nothing.  At `m = 1` those\nclauses are vacuous, and §11 states `K₁ ≥ 2` outright — it is the middle conjunct of case (i),\n`M = 1, K₁ ≥ 2, b ≤ K₁^{s₁t}`.  `RothGammaFull` encodes case (ii) only, so at `m = 1` it was\nnot `Γ₁`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.heightBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.heightBound","kind":"theorem","line":859,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L859-L859","doc":"Clause 4, raised to `m·q` so that it is integral. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.floor","kind":"theorem","line":857,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L857-L857","doc":"Clause 3b: the first **height** is at least `2^F`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.floorExp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.floorExp","kind":"theorem","line":855,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L855-L855","doc":"Clause 3a: the floor's exponent is large enough, cleared of division. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.height","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.height","kind":"theorem","line":852,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L852-L852","doc":"Clause 2: the first variable's `K^s` is the smallest. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.growth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.growth","kind":"theorem","line":850,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L850-L850","doc":"Clause 1, with `ω = 1/t = q/p`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull.mk","kind":"ctor","line":847,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L847-L847","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGammaFull","kind":"structure","line":836,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L836-L877","doc":"**Property `Γ_M`, case (ii), in full** — all four clauses, in the `t = p/q` idiom.\n\n`RothGamma` above carries clauses 1–3 with `ω : ℚ` and leaves clause 4 to `RothLemmaUniform`.\nThis is the faithful object: `t = p/q`, so `ω = 1/t = q/p`, and both of the clauses that had a\nrational exponent become `ℕ` inequalities with nothing rounded.\n\n* **clause 3** is `2^F ≤ K₁` together with `q·(M−1)M(2M+1) ≤ F·p`, which is\n  `K₁ ≥ 2^{(1/t)(M−1)M(2M+1)}` cleared of division.  No `Nat.ceil`, so no slack to lose.\n* **clause 4** is `b^{Mq} ≤ K₁^{s₁p}`, which is `b ≤ K₁^{(1/M)s₁t}` raised to `Mq`.\n\n⚠ Still case (ii) only; `Γ₁` is case (i) and is not this. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGamma_first_two_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGamma_first_two_le","kind":"theorem","line":828,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L828-L834","doc":"**`K₁ ≥ 2` is a consequence of `Γ_m`, not a hypothesis** — §11 says so, and this is the\nfirst index's case.  `RothLemmaUniform`'s standing `2 ≤ qⱼ` is therefore redundant at `j = 0`\nrather than additional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_two_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_two_le","kind":"theorem","line":811,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L811-L826","doc":"**`K₁ ≥ 2` is forced by clause 3** once `m ≥ 2` and `ω > 0`.\n\nThe floor's exponent `ω·(m−1)m(2m+1)` is then at least `10ω > 0`, so its ceiling is at least\n`1` and the floor is at least `2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGamma.floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGamma.floor","kind":"theorem","line":809,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L809-L809","doc":"Clause 3: the first **height** is at least `2^{ω(m−1)m(2m+1)}`.  See correction\neighteen at `rothHeightFloor`: `K_h` is `H_h`, not `Q_h`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGamma.height","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGamma.height","kind":"theorem","line":805,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L805-L805","doc":"Clause 2: the first variable's `K^s` is the smallest.  `s_h·log K_h ≥ s₁·log K₁`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGamma.growth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGamma.growth","kind":"theorem","line":803,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L803-L803","doc":"Clause 1: consecutive degrees drop by at least a factor `ω = 1/t`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGamma.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGamma.mk","kind":"ctor","line":800,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L800-L800","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGamma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGamma","kind":"structure","line":766,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L766-L809","doc":"**Property `Γ_m`** (§11) — clauses 1–3 of case (ii).\n\n⚠ **`Γ_M` is a disjunction with four clauses, not a conjunction of three.**  §11 defines\n`b, s₁,…,s_M, K₁,…,K_M` to have `Γ_M` if **either**\n\n    (i)   M = 1,  K₁ ≥ 2,  b ≤ K₁^{s₁t};\n\n    (ii)  M ≥ 2  and  simultaneously\n          1.  max(s₂/s₁, …, s_M/s_{M−1}) ≤ t      — `growth`, `RothGrowth` with `ω = 1/t`\n          2.  s_h·log K_h ≥ s₁·log K₁  (h = 1..M) — `height`, written multiplicatively\n          3.  K₁ ≥ 2^{(1/t)(M−1)M(2M+1)}          — `floor`\n          4.  b ≤ K₁^{(1/M)s₁t}                   — ⚠ **not a field here**\n\nAn earlier version of this docstring said \"all three clauses\", which is wrong twice over.\nWhere the rest lives:\n\n* **clause 4** is `RothLemmaUniform`'s height condition — `∀ j, B ≤ qⱼ^E` with `E ≤ ε·dⱼ` and\n  `ε = t/m`, which is `b ≤ K₁^{(1/M)s₁t}` in uniform form.  It is carried, just not here.\n* **case (i)** is the `m = 1` specialisation.  At `m = 1` the three fields below are vacuous or\n  trivial (`rothHeightFloor 1 ω = 1`), and `RothLemmaUniform`'s standing `2 ≤ qⱼ` supplies\n  `K₁ ≥ 2`.  So the aggregate matches, but this structure alone is **not** `Γ₁`.\n\nClause 2 is stated as `K₁^{s₁} ≤ K_h^{s_h}`, the same inequality with the logarithms cleared —\nwhich is **exactly** Bombieri–Gubler 6.3.4's condition `qⱼ^{dⱼ} ≥ q₁^{d₁}`.  §11 runs it over\n`h = 1,…,M`; Theorem 1 states it for `h = 2,…,m`.  The two agree, since `h = 1` is trivial, and\nthe `∀ j k, (j : ℕ) = 0 → …` encoding below covers both.\n\n⚠ `K_h ≥ 2` is a **consequence** of `Γ_M`, not a hypothesis — §11 derives it, along with\n`s₁ ≥ … ≥ s_M` and `∑ s_h ≤ M·s₁`.  `rothGamma_first_two_le` recovers the first of those here.\n`RothLemmaUniform` assumes `2 ≤ qⱼ` outright, which is therefore redundant rather than\nadditional.\n\nBoth indexed clauses are written `∀ j, (j : ℕ) = 0 → …` rather than through `o.symm 0`, so that\n`m = 0` needs no side condition rather than no `Fin 0` element. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_mono","kind":"theorem","line":751,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L751-L764","doc":"**The floor is monotone in the arity.**\n\nThis is what lets `Γ_m` hand its floor clause down to `Γ_{m−1}`: the sub-system asks for a\n*smaller* floor than the system supplies, because `(m−1)m(2m+1)` grows with `m`.  Without it\nthe descent (`RothVariableSplit.rothGamma_descend`) would have nothing to discharge clause 3\nwith. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_one","kind":"theorem","line":747,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L747-L749","doc":"At one variable the floor is `1`, so it constrains nothing beyond `Γ₁`'s own `K₁ ≥ 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor","kind":"def","line":728,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L728-L745","doc":"**Mahler's height floor**, `Γ_m`'s third clause: `K₁ ≥ 2^{(1/t)(m−1)m(2m+1)}`.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction eighteen: `K_h` is the HEIGHT `H_h`, not the denominator.**  This definition\nwas called `rothDenomFloor` and its clause \"the first *denominator*\".  §11 fixes the reading\nbeyond argument — after defining `Γ_M` abstractly on `b, s₁,…,s_M, K₁,…,K_M` it says:\n\n> By way of application, let `m ≥ 2`; let the ordered system of numbers `a, r₁,…,r_m, H₁,…,H_m`\n> have the property `Γ_m`\n\nso `b = a`, `s_h = r_h`, and `K_h = H_h`.  §13's descent confirms it: the new system is\n`b, ρ₁,…,ρ_{m−1}, H₁,…,H_{m−1}` — the same heights, truncated — and its clauses are written\n`ρ_h log H_h ≥ ρ₁ log H₁` and `H₁ ≥ 2^{(1/t)(m−1)m(2m+1)}`, in `H` throughout.\n\nThe exponent `ω·(m−1)m(2m+1)` is rational, so this rounds it **up** (`Nat.ceil`).  That makes\nthe clause stronger than the source's and hence every statement carrying it weaker — the safe\ndirection for a hypothesis being reinstated, since it cannot let a claim here outrun §14's. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.le_arity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.le_arity","kind":"theorem","line":711,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L711-L719","doc":"**`Θ_m ≤ m` in the arc's arity language.**\n\n`IsRothTheta.le_card` bounds by `Fintype.card σ`; everywhere else in this arc the arity is\ncarried by an ordering `o : σ ≃ Fin m`, and those agree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.le_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.le_card","kind":"theorem","line":694,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L694-L709","doc":"**`Θ_m ≤ m`** — §9's third property, and the last of the three.\n\nNeeds `degreeOf_translate` (via `RothIndex.index_le_card`): the index is an infimum over the\n*translated* support, so `Γ`'s degree bound on `A` reaches it only because translation\npreserves degree.  That is why this could not be stated before `degreeOf_translate` existed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_map_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_map_le","kind":"theorem","line":687,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L687-L692","doc":"`map` along a ring hom cannot raise a degree — its support only shrinks. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.mono_height","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.mono_height","kind":"theorem","line":675,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L675-L685","doc":"**`Θ_m` is non-decreasing in `a`** — §9's second property.\n\n`Θ_m` is *attained* at some `A ∈ R(a;r)`, and `R(a;r) ⊆ R(a';r)`, so the larger problem's upper\nbound applies to that very `A`.  ⚠ Attainment is doing the work: without it one would have to\ncompare two suprema, and the inclusion alone would not suffice to transfer a witness. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.InRothPolySet.mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.InRothPolySet.mono","kind":"theorem","line":670,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L670-L673","doc":"`R(a; r)` only grows with `a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_le_of_gamma_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_le_of_gamma_one","kind":"theorem","line":628,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L628-L649","doc":"**`Γ₁` closes the base case: `Θ₁ ≤ t`.**\n\n§11's clause (i) is `M = 1, K₁ ≥ 2, b ≤ K₁^{s₁t}`.  With correction eighteen's reading\n(`K₁ = H₁`, `b = a`, `s₁ = r₁`) and `t = p/q`, that is `a^q ≤ H^{r·p}`, and §10 turns it into\n`Θ₁ ≤ t` exactly.\n\n⚠ **This is where §10's `P^j` half is load-bearing.**  The `Q^j` half alone gives\n`Θ₁ ≤ log a/(r log q)`, and clause (i) then only bounds that by `t·log H/log q ≥ t` — the wrong\nside of `t`, by exactly the factor the two halves differ in.  The arc carried the `q`-form for\na long time and could not close the base case; the reason was this factor, not any missing\nrecursion. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_le_of_pow_pow_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_le_of_pow_pow_le","kind":"theorem","line":614,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L614-L626","doc":"**§10 in `Θ` form, powered: `a^Q ≤ H^S` gives `Θ₁ ≤ S/(Q·r)`.**\n\nThe shape `Γ₁` delivers.  §11's clause (i) is `b ≤ K₁^{s₁t}` for rational `t = p/q`; clearing\nthe exponent gives `b^q ≤ K₁^{s₁p}` with no rounding anywhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_le_of_pow_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_le_of_pow_le","kind":"theorem","line":596,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L596-L612","doc":"**§10 in `Θ` form: `a ≤ H^e` and `H ≥ 2` give `Θ₁ ≤ e/r`.**\n\nMahler states this as `Θ₁(a; r; H) ≤ log a / (r log H)`; `e` is a root-free stand-in for\n`log a / log H`, kept exponential because the recursion amplifies any rounding\n(`two_sqrt_expands`).\n\n⚠ `2 ≤ H` is necessary, not cosmetic: at `H = 1` the right-hand side of Mahler's form is\ninfinite and the estimate is empty. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_polynomial_of_isRothTheta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_polynomial_of_isRothTheta","kind":"theorem","line":553,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L553-L594","doc":"**`IsRothTheta`'s realising pair, transported to `ℤ[X]`.**\n\nEverything §10 needs about the maximising `(A, κ)`, in the one-variable dictionary: a\npolynomial, a point in lowest terms, the height identity `max(|p|,|q|) = H`, the coefficient\nbound, and the index *equal* to `Θ`.\n\nCoprimality and `0 < q` are not hypotheses anywhere — Lean's `ℚ` carries `reduced` and a\npositive denominator by construction, which is why `InRothFracSet` needs no coprimality clause\neven though §9 states one.\n\nExtracted so that the unpowered and powered `Θ` bounds share it; they differ only in which\nindex theorem they call. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.rename_equiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.rename_equiv","kind":"theorem","line":502,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L502-L529","doc":"**`IsRothTheta` transports along a type equivalence.**  The value `Θ` is unchanged — it is a\nsupremum over sets the equivalence matches up. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.InRothFracSet.rename_equiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.InRothFracSet.rename_equiv","kind":"theorem","line":498,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L498-L500","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.InRothPolySet.rename_equiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.InRothPolySet.rename_equiv","kind":"theorem","line":481,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L481-L496","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_isRothTheta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_isRothTheta","kind":"theorem","line":455,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L455-L468","doc":"**§9's existence obligation, discharged.**\n\n⚠ The hypotheses are only `1 ≤ a` and `1 ≤ Hₕ`, and both are **necessary** rather than\nconvenient: `a ≤ 0` empties `R(a;r)` and `Hₕ = 0` empties `Q(H)`, and an empty set has no\nmaximum to attain.  Nothing about finiteness is assumed — it is proved.\n\nThis is what the section above said was \"an undischarged obligation … known satisfiable\nbecause the two sets are finite\".  It is now discharged, so `IsRothTheta` is a characterisation\nof something that exists, and `IsRothTheta.unique` makes it *the* `Θ_m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_isRothTheta_of_finite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_isRothTheta_of_finite","kind":"theorem","line":421,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L421-L453","doc":"**`Θ_m` exists** once both sets are finite and non-empty.\n\nThe index of any `A ∈ R(a;r)` at any `κ` is a genuine rational rather than `⊤`: `index = ⊤`\nhappens only for the zero polynomial, and `A ≠ 0` survives `map (algebraMap ℤ ℚ)`.  So the\nindex is a `WithTop ℚ`-valued function on a finite non-empty product, which attains a maximum;\nuntopping it gives a rational satisfying both clauses — the upper bound from maximality, the\nattainment from the maximiser.\n\n⚠ No `Fintype σ` and no positivity on `r`: `index ≠ ⊤` follows from `A ≠ 0` alone through\n`index_eq_top_iff`, so the route via `index_le_card` (which needs both) is not required. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_inRothFracSet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_inRothFracSet","kind":"theorem","line":407,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L407-L419","doc":"`Q(H)` is non-empty as soon as every `H h` is at least `1`: take `κ h = Hₕ`, an integer,\nwhose numerator is `Hₕ` and whose denominator is `1`.\n\n⚠ `1 ≤ Hₕ` is necessary — every rational has `den ≥ 1`, so its height is at least `1`.\n⚠ With correction sixteen the witness can no longer be `κ ≡ 0`: that has height `1`, which is\nthe required height only when `Hₕ = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_inRothPolySet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_inRothPolySet","kind":"theorem","line":392,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L392-L405","doc":"`R(a; r)` is non-empty as soon as `1 ≤ a`: the constant polynomial `1` works.\n\n⚠ `1 ≤ a` is not merely convenient, it is **necessary** — a non-zero `A` has a non-zero\ncoefficient, so `1 ≤ |coeff n A| ≤ a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.finite_rothFracSet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.finite_rothFracSet","kind":"theorem","line":381,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L381-L390","doc":"**`Q(H₁,…,H_m)` is finite** — it is exactly the `Set.pi` of the bounded-height sets. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.finite_setOf_rat_height_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.finite_setOf_rat_height_le","kind":"theorem","line":363,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L363-L379","doc":"**Rationals of bounded height form a finite set.**\n\nA rational is determined by `(num, den)` (`Rat.ext`), and the bound confines that pair to\n`Icc (-n) n ×ˢ Iic n`.  ⚠ The finiteness comes entirely from the height bound, not from the\ntype. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.finite_rothPolySet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.finite_rothPolySet","kind":"theorem","line":358,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L358-L361","doc":"**`R(a; r)` is finite.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.finite_setOf_degreeOf_le_coeff_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.finite_setOf_degreeOf_le_coeff_le","kind":"theorem","line":325,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L325-L356","doc":"**Bounded degree plus bounded height is a finite set of polynomials.**\n\n⚠ Stated *without* `A ≠ 0`, which is the honest form: nothing in the argument uses it.  ⚠ And\nwith no sign hypothesis on `a`: for `a < 0` the height clause is unsatisfiable and the set is\nempty, which is finite for a reason the proof never has to distinguish. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.finite_setOf_forall_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.finite_setOf_forall_le","kind":"theorem","line":316,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L316-L323","doc":"**The exponent box is finite.**  Over a finite variable type, the monomials whose exponent\nin each variable `j` is at most `r j` form a finite set.\n\n⚠ `Finite σ` is essential as soon as some `r j ≠ 0`: over an infinite `σ` the box for `r ≡ 1`\ncontains every squarefree monomial, of which there are infinitely many. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_degenerate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_degenerate","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L265-L303","doc":"⚠ **`IsRothTheta` is satisfiable as stated** — a degenerate but genuine instance.\n\nAt `r ≡ 0` every `idxWeight` term is `(i j)/0 = 0`, so `idxWeight` vanishes identically and the\nindex of *any* non-zero polynomial is `0`.  Both clauses then hold with `Θ = 0`, attained at\n`A = 1`, `κ ≡ 0`.\n\n⚠ **Superseded by `exists_isRothTheta`**, which gives inhabitation at every admissible\nparameter.  Kept because it is what was available before that: a cheap check that the\ntwo-clause `∀`/`∃` definition is not self-contradictory.  This arc has landed such a definition\nbefore — correction 10's `hres` was unsatisfiable and looked fine — and at the time this was\nthe only evidence there was. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":254,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L254-L263","doc":"**`0 ≤ Θ_m`** — the index is a sum of non-negative terms, and `Θ_m` is attained. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta.unique","kind":"theorem","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L244-L252","doc":"**The characterisation pins the value.**  So `IsRothTheta` may be carried as a hypothesis\nwithout ambiguity, and any later construction agrees with it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.IsRothTheta","kind":"def","line":236,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L236-L242","doc":"**`Θ_m` characterised** — the least upper bound of `J(A; r; κ)` over `R(a;r) × Q(H)`, and\nattained.  §9's finiteness is what makes the second clause available. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.InRothFracSet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.InRothFracSet","kind":"def","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L218-L234","doc":"`Q(H₁,…,H_m)` — systems of rationals **of height exactly** `Hₕ`.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction sixteen: this was `≤`, and that made `RothLemmaTheta` false.**  §9 says\n\"systems … **of heights** `Hₕ = max(|Pₕ|,|Qₕ|)`\" — an equality.  With `≤` the set is larger,\nso `Θ_m` is a supremum over more, and §10's bound fails outright: at `H = 100`, `a = 2`,\n`r = 1`, the point `κ = 1/1` has height `1 ≤ 100` and `A = x − 1` has index `1`, against\n`log a/(r log H) = log 2 / log 100 ≈ 0.15`.  With the equality, `κ` must have height `100`, so\n`A` would need `|lead| ≥ 100 > a` and the index is `0`.\n\nThe failure mode is the arc's familiar one — a *weakened hypothesis* makes the statement\nstronger, and a statement stronger than the source's is one the source will not prove.  Same\nshape as `Γ_m`'s missing clauses, in a definition landed three turns ago.\n\n⚠ Mahler also requires `(Pₕ, Qₕ) = 1`; Lean's `ℚ` is always in lowest terms, so that clause is\ncarried by the type rather than stated. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.InRothPolySet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.InRothPolySet","kind":"def","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L214-L216","doc":"`R(a; r₁,…,r_m)` — non-zero integer polynomials of degree `≤ rₕ` in `xₕ`, height `≤ a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_scale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_scale","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L173-L185","doc":"**Rescaling the degree vector preserves the growth condition.**\n\nThe induction step measures its sub-problems against `r · d` rather than `d` (the source's\n`p_h = n·r_h`), so the growth condition has to survive that rescaling.  It does, and for the\nobvious reason: the factor `r` appears on both sides. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_row_cost_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_row_cost_le","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L149-L171","doc":"**`t ≤ 1/ω`** — the row cost of the induction step, bounded by the growth constant alone.\n\n`d_last/d_earlier ≤ 1/ω`, straight from `rothGrowth_mul_le`.  So `ω ≥ 1/ε` forces `t ≤ ε`.\n\n⚠ The `t` here is the *row cost*, a local quantity; do not confuse it with §11's parameter\n`t`, against which `ω = 1/t` — under that identification this bound reads `row cost ≤ t`,\nwhich is the shape §13 consumes, on the nose.\n\n⚠ This used to be stated with numerator `d_last + 1` and conclusion `2/ω`, the `2` being\n`1 + 1/d_last ≤ 2`.  Both are true, and the loose one is what the arc carried for eleven\ncommits; §13's row term is `(μ-1)/r_{m-1}` with `μ ≤ n ≤ r_m + 1`, so the numerator the\nsource uses is `r_m`, not `r_m + 1`.  Correction 24. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_mul_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_mul_le","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L133-L147","doc":"**Every earlier degree is at least `ω` times any later one.**\n\nThe single-step condition plus antitonicity; this is the form the row cost needs, because the\ndegree floor `D₁` ranges over *all* the non-split variables, not just the adjacent one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_antitone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_antitone","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L112-L131","doc":"Under `RothGrowth` with `ω ≥ 1`, degrees are non-increasing along the ordering. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothGrowth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothGrowth","kind":"def","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L85-L88","doc":"The growth condition on the degree vector, read along a chosen ordering of the variables:\nconsecutive degrees drop by at least a factor `ω`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.symm_map_pUnitAlgEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.symm_map_pUnitAlgEquiv","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L75-L81","doc":"The shape `index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le` expects, recovered from a plain\n`MvPolynomial Unit ℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pUnitAlgEquiv_map","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pUnitAlgEquiv_map","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothLemmaStatement.lean#L67-L73","doc":"`pUnitAlgEquiv` commutes with coefficient maps. ","signatureChunk":0}]}